-- A parameter whose *value* is an address, and the return roots it can and cannot supply.
--
-- The two shapes are `&T` and `%T`, and they have the same standing here for the same reason: the
-- address arrives in a register, so the body's copy of it is its own, but the storage it names is
-- still the caller's. That is what `return` has always been about, so both may carry the marker and
-- both are reported without it. Neither used to be either - a parameter with no slot in this frame
-- had no provenance at all, so a result built out of one was rooted in nothing and the check it
-- should have failed never ran. See Provenance::args and arrivesAsCopy.
--
-- ReferenceRoot.yana is the accepting half: the same signatures with the markers written.
--
-- The reference cases are written with **tickless** parameters, and that follows from the
-- loan-group rule rather than from taste: a tick on a parameter's type is itself the declaration
-- that the storage is the caller's (Analysis-Borrows.md §3.1 and §8.2), so `x: 'Int` is a declared
-- root and there is no way left to write an undeclared one. `x: Cell` is §3.1's ordinary parameter
-- and a reference reached through it is what these return. A `%T` keeps carrying itself, because a
-- raw pointer is outside the ownership model and has no tick to declare anything with.

import Native

data Pair(a, b) {first: a, second: b}
data Cell {value: Int}

-- Repackaging into a generic aggregate does not launder the references it carries. Both arguments
-- are roots of the result, and neither is declared.
fn pairOf(x: Cell, y: Cell) -> Pair('Int, 'Int) = Pair {first: x.value, second: y.value}

-- The same one level down, where the result is a reference rather than a record holding two.
fn firstOf(x: Cell, y: Cell) -> 'Int = x.value

-- A pointer handed straight back. Address arithmetic on it is the same answer: `p + 1` stays inside
-- whatever `p` named.
fn passThrough(p: %U8) -> %U8 = p

fn offsetOf(p: %U8) -> %U8 = p + (1 :: Size)

-- And a reference reached through a nested type, so the result is not itself borrow-shaped.
fn maybeOf(x: Cell) -> Maybe('Int) = Just(x.value)

fn main() -> Int = 0
